                                       has NO entry in `show ipv6 neighbors` either, so
                                       even its neighbour solicitation was never answered.

  TWO DISPLAY BEHAVIOURS MEASURED HERE.  Both would trip a parser written for IPv4.
  Neither is a defect.

  (a) FOR AN IPv6 SERVER, `Reference ID` IS A HASH, NOT THE ADDRESS.  The DUT prints
      `Reference ID : 09D7146F (fd32:b1f0:dff8:1603::1)`.  09D7146F is not any part of
      that address.  The NTP reference-identifier field is 32 bits wide and an IPv6
      address is 128 bits, so NTPv4 puts a hash there instead.  Only the part inside the
      brackets is the address.  Anyone who grades an IPv6 NTP case by matching the hex
      against the configured address will read a correct device as wrong.

  (b) `show ntp associations` WRAPS AN IPv6 REMOTE ONTO ITS OWN LINE.  The `remote`
      column is sized for an IPv4 address.  An IPv6 address is longer, so the refid,
      stratum, reach, delay and offset for that peer print on the FOLLOWING line.  The
      `*` system-peer marker stays on the address line.  A regex that expects one line,
      such as `^\* <address>.*reach`, matches nothing even though the device is working.
      Read the pair of lines, not one line.

  => STEP 2 PASS: the DUT chose the reachable IPv6 server.  It resolved that server on
     the correct link.  It reached Stratum 6 from a stratum-5 source and reported itself
     synchronised, with a microsecond offset and a settled 64.7 s poll interval.  It never
     used the unreachable IPv6 control address: reach 0, no system-peer marker, and the
     poll interval backing off from 128 s to 256 s.

