awplus#@sleep 100
awplus#show clock
UTC Time:   Wed, 21 Oct 2026 23:19:07 +0000     <- box UTC at this moment: 23:19:59
awplus#show ntp status
   ... identical: Stratum 0, Reference ID 00000000 (), Ref time 1970,
       Leap status : Not synchronised
awplus#show ntp associations
remote          refid           st t when poll reach    delay   offset    disp
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  fd32:b1f0:dff8:1603::99 
                  -                0 u    -   64     0        -        -       -
  fd32:b1f0:dff8:1603::1 
                  10.32.16.2       5 u   43   64   377    0.511 50914.20   0.002
* system peer, # backup, + candidate, - outlier, x false ticker, @ dynamic

  100 SECONDS LATER THE CLOCK IS STILL WRONG BY THE SAME AMOUNT.  The IPv6 association
  is in perfect health at the same time: `reach 377` means the last eight polls were all
  answered, which is the maximum.  The server is at stratum 5 with 0.511 ms delay.  The
  DUT is receiving good IPv6 time, and it REPORTS the error exactly: `offset 50914.20`
  milliseconds.  It has also dropped the `*` system-peer marker from that server.
  The real injected error is 50.914 s, not the 45 s aimed for.  The 45 s was computed
  from the box's clock a few seconds before the command was typed and logged in.  The
  size that matters is the one the DUT itself measures, and that is the one used below.

  MEASURED BEHAVIOUR: A RUNNING TIME DAEMON WILL NOT STEP A CLOCK THAT `clock set` MOVED
  UNDER IT.  It keeps polling, it keeps being answered, and it keeps reporting the true
  offset, but it leaves the clock wrong.  Anyone who sets the clock by hand on a
  synchronised AW+ device and then waits for NTP to put it back will wait without end.

