  THE DUT'S OWN LOG, which is the primary evidence for this step:
awplus#show log | include chronyd
2026 Oct 21 23:11:41 daemon.warning awplus chronyd[22637]: System clock wrong by 2.241471 seconds
2026 Oct 21 23:11:43 daemon.warning awplus chronyd[22637]: System clock was stepped by 2.241471 seconds
2026 Oct 21 23:17:26 daemon.warning awplus chronyd[22637]: Backward time jump detected!
2026 Oct 21 23:19:48 daemon.warning awplus chronyd[23156]: System clock wrong by 50.914095 seconds
2026 Oct 21 23:20:39 daemon.warning awplus chronyd[23156]: System clock was stepped by 50.914095 seconds

  Reading the log line by line:
   - 23:11:41 and 23:11:43, process 22637: the step-2 correction.  A real 2.241471 s
     error, measured and removed 3 s after `ntp server fd32:b1f0:dff8:1603::1` was
     applied, entirely over IPv6.
   - 23:17:26, process 22637: "Backward time jump detected!".  The daemon SAW the
     `clock set`, and still did not undo it.
   - 23:19:48 and 23:20:39, process 23156: THE PROCESS ID HAS CHANGED.  The
     `no ntp server ...` then `ntp server ...` pair restarted the time daemon.  The fresh
     daemon measured the error as 50.914095 s and STEPPED THE CLOCK BY 50.914095 s.
   - The last two lines look 51 s apart and are not.  The "wrong by" line was written
     while the clock was still 51 s slow.  The "stepped by" line was written straight
     afterwards, on the corrected clock.  They are the same real instant.

  THE CROSS-CHECK THAT MAKES THIS A MEASUREMENT.  Two separate readings of the same error
  agree to about a tenth of a millisecond:
      `show ntp associations` offset ... 50914.20 ms   (the DUT's NTP view, before)
      `chronyd ... stepped by  50.914095 seconds`      (the DUT's system log, after)

  TIMING.  The client was restarted at 23:20:36.  The time daemon stepped the clock at
  23:20:39, which is 3 s later.  The DUT read `Leap status : Normal` at Stratum 6 on the
  first poll at 23:20:48, which is 12 s later.

